    "DESCRIPTION": "FIND: `DetectorHealthMonitor.record_round()` never increments `_pathology_counts[\"mu\"]` on the pathological branch of Check 2 (`mu increasing while finding count stable/declining`). Location: `record_round()`, Check 2. Evidence: when `mu_increasing and findings_not_increasing` is true, the code emits a `DetectorDiagnosis` but does not update `_pathology_counts[\"mu\"]`; the `else` branch later checks `if self._pathology_counts.get(\"mu\", 0) > 0` to resolve/reset it. This makes mu persistence always appear as zero, breaks adaptive-window persistence input for `effective_window(\"mu\", ...)`, prevents natural-resolution accounting from ever firing for actual mu incidents, and defeats any logic expecting recurring mu distortion to escalate over time. This is distinct from prior findings about missing `pathology_key` or signed/absolute mu comparisons: the persistent-state machine for mu is simply not advanced.",
    "PROPOSED_FIX": "FIND-FIX-FOLLOW\nFIND: In `DetectorHealthMonitor.record_round()`, Check 2 emits a mu pathology diagnosis without incrementing `self._pathology_counts[\"mu\"]`. The resolution path explicitly reads that key, so the pathology can never become persistent or resolve from a nonzero state. Evidence: pathological branch constructs `DetectorDiagnosis(...)` directly; no `self._pathology_counts[\"mu\"] = ...` occurs before append.\n\nFIX:\n<<<<\n            if mu_increasing and findings_not_increasing:\n                # Check if model count changed (benching distortion)\n                recent_models = self._active_model_counts[-(eff_mu_window + 1):]\n                model_count_changed = len(set(recent_models)) > 1\n\n                diag = DetectorDiagnosis(\n                    detector=\"mu\",\n                    pathology=f\"mu increasing ({recent_mu[0]:.1f} → {recent_mu[-1]:.1f}) \"\n                              f\"while findings stable/declining ({recent_findings})\",\n                    severity=\"WARNING\" if not model_count_changed else \"CRITICAL\",\n                    recommended_action=(\n                        \"Cost distortion from model benching/unbenching. mu = delta_Y / c_r \"\n                        \"is unreliable when active model count changes. Use novelty_rate as \"\n                        \"primary convergence signal.\"\n                        if model_count_changed\n                        else \"mu may be unreliable — check cost calculation and yield function.\"\n                    ),\n                    evidence={\n                        \"mu_values\": recent_mu,\n                        \"finding_counts\": recent_findings,\n                        \"active_models\": recent_models,\n                        \"model_count_changed\": model_count_changed,\n                    },\n                )\n                new_diagnoses.append(diag)\n>>>>\n====\n            if mu_increasing and findings_not_increasing:\n                # Check if model count changed (benching distortion)\n                recent_models = self._active_model_counts[-(eff_mu_window + 1):]\n                model_count_changed = len(set(recent_models)) > 1\n                persistence = self._pathology_counts.get(\"mu\", 0)\n                self._pathology_counts[\"mu\"] = persistence + 1\n\n                diag = DetectorDiagnosis(\n                    detector=\"mu\",\n                    pathology=f\"mu increasing ({recent_mu[0]:.1f} → {recent_mu[-1]:.1f}) \"\n                              f\"while findings stable/declining ({recent_findings})\",\n                    severity=(\n                        \"CRITICAL\"\n                        if model_count_changed or persistence >= 1\n                        else \"WARNING\"\n                    ),\n                    recommended_action=(\n                        \"Cost distortion from model benching/unbenching. mu = delta_Y / c_r \"\n                        \"is unreliable when active model count changes. Use novelty_rate as \"\n                        \"primary convergence signal.\"\n                        if model_count_changed\n                        else \"mu may be unreliable — check cost calculation and yield function.\"\n                    ),\n                    evidence={\n                        \"mu_values\": recent_mu,\n                        \"finding_counts\": recent_findings,\n                        \"active_models\": recent_models,\n                        \"model_count_changed\": model_count_changed,\n                        \"persistence\": persistence + 1,\n                        \"adaptive_window\": eff_mu_window,\n                    },\n                    pathology_key=\"mu_distortion\",\n                )\n                new_diagnoses.append(diag)\n>>>>\nFOLLOW: This fix changes downstream behavior in three places. (1) `effective_window(\"mu\", ...)` will now receive a nonzero persistent count, so its current formula will actively reshape the mu detection window; because prior findings already established that this formula has directional/calibration defects, enabling persistence makes those defects operational instead of latent. (2) The `else` branch’s natural-resolution path (`record_natural_resolution(\"mu\")`) will now execute for real mu incidents, which will feed `false_positive_rate`; because the denominator logic there is already approximate, this fix may increase self-diagnosis pressure and expose the false-positive-rate mismeasurement more often. (3) `apply_diagnosis()` and any remediation routing using `pathology_key` must accept `\"mu_distortion\"`; if `_REMEDIATION_CHAINS` lacks that key or uses a different name, this reveals/activates the existing chain-definition completeness issue rather than creating a new one.",
