    "DESCRIPTION": "FIND: `check_dispatch_health()` computes verification-miscalibration statistics over the full lifetime history instead of a recent window, so one early bad phase can permanently poison later rounds and vice versa. Location: `check_dispatch_health()`, verification-miscalibration block. Evidence: for each model it uses `m = sum(rates) / len(rates)` over the entire list, and `rates` comes from `verification_rates: model_id -> list of per-round verification rates`. There is no `round_idx`-scoped slicing. Immune decisions are supposed to be adaptive and round-local; using all history makes current pathology detection laggy and insensitive to recent drift. This is distinct from the already-known issue about z-score calibration and whether means vs observations are compared.",
    "PROPOSED_FIX": "FIND-FIX-FOLLOW\nFIND: `check_dispatch_health()` uses all historical verification rates when diagnosing current-round miscalibration. This violates temporal locality for an adaptive monitor and can hide current drift behind stale history. Evidence: `m = sum(rates) / len(rates)` with no use of `round_idx` other than being passed into the function.\n\nFIX:\n<<<<\n        all_rates: List[float] = []\n        model_means: Dict[str, float] = {}\n        for model_id, rates in verification_rates.items():\n            if rates:\n                m = sum(rates) / len(rates)\n                model_means[model_id] = m\n                all_rates.extend(rates)\n>>>>\n====\n        all_rates: List[float] = []\n        model_means: Dict[str, float] = {}\n        window = max(3, self._config.persistence_window if self._config else 3)\n        for model_id, rates in verification_rates.items():\n            recent_rates = rates[-window:]\n            if recent_rates:\n                m = sum(recent_rates) / len(recent_rates)\n                model_means[model_id] = m\n                all_rates.extend(recent_rates)\n>>>>\n\n<<<<\n                            evidence={\n                                \"model_id\": model_id,\n                                \"model_mean\": m,\n                                \"population_mean\": pop_mean,\n                                \"population_std\": pop_std,\n                                \"z_score\": z_score,\n                                \"occurrence\": persistence + 1,\n                            },\n>>>>\n====\n                            evidence={\n                                \"model_id\": model_id,\n                                \"model_mean\": m,\n                                \"population_mean\": pop_mean,\n                                \"population_std\": pop_std,\n                                \"z_score\": z_score,\n                                \"occurrence\": persistence + 1,\n                                \"window\": window,\n                                \"round_idx\": round_idx,\n                            },\n>>>>\nFOLLOW: This fix changes the semantics of persistence in verification diagnoses. (1) `_pathology_counts[f\"verif_miscal_{model_id}\"]` will now reflect recent-window anomalies rather than lifetime-average anomalies, so repeated diagnoses may become less sticky but more faithful. (2) If tests currently assert behavior based on cumulative averages, they must be updated to recent-window behavior. (3) This fix does not resolve the separate threshold-calibration issue for small N; it only makes the statistic time-local.",
