    "DESCRIPTION": "FIND: `FailureHandler.get_recovery()` mutates internal `role_map` on `DOWNGRADE_ROLE`, but there is no corresponding public contract to propagate the downgrade to the orchestrator’s authoritative role assignment object. The interface summary explicitly marks `role_assignment.record_failure(...)` as an external function from `process_round()`, but no immune-layer API returns a role-change delta or invokes a callback for role changes. Evidence from visible code: `elif action == RecoveryAction.DOWNGRADE_ROLE: ... self.role_map[model_id] = Role.PAR` is an in-place mutation on a plain dict. This assumes shared ownership of the same mapping object between `FailureHandler` and the rest of the system, which is an implicit aliasing contract, not an explicit interface. This is adjacent to but narrower than the known finding about `RoleAssignment`/`LoadBalancer` propagation: the missed defect is that the boundary contract itself is undefined and fail-open.",
    "PROPOSED_FIX": "FIND-FIX-FOLLOW\nFIND: `FailureHandler` communicates role downgrade via hidden shared mutable aliasing (`role_map` dict mutation) instead of an explicit interface. If the caller passes a copy, or if `RoleAssignment` is authoritative elsewhere, the downgrade silently does not propagate. Evidence: there is no callback, return payload, or event carrying the new role.\n\nFIX:\n<<<<\n    def get_recovery(\n        self,\n        model_id: str,\n        round_idx: int,\n        failure_type: FailureType,\n    ) -> RecoveryAction:\n>>>>\n====\n    def get_recovery(\n        self,\n        model_id: str,\n        round_idx: int,\n        failure_type: FailureType,\n    ) -> Tuple[RecoveryAction, Optional[Role]]:\n>>>>\n\n<<<<\n            self._record_failure(model_id, round_idx, failure_type, action)\n            return action\n>>>>\n====\n            self._record_failure(model_id, round_idx, failure_type, action)\n            return action, None\n>>>>\n\n<<<<\n        self._record_failure(model_id, round_idx, failure_type, action)\n\n        # Execute exclusion if needed\n        if action == RecoveryAction.EXCLUDE:\n            self._active_models.discard(model_id)\n        # IM_F008: Execute role downgrade — update role_map so subsequent\n        # _expected_performance() calls use the correct baseline vector.\n        elif action == RecoveryAction.DOWNGRADE_ROLE:\n            current_role = self.role_map.get(model_id)\n            if current_role == Role.COL:\n                self.role_map[model_id] = Role.PAR\n\n        return action\n>>>>\n====\n        self._record_failure(model_id, round_idx, failure_type, action)\n\n        new_role: Optional[Role] = None\n        if action == RecoveryAction.EXCLUDE:\n            self._active_models.discard(model_id)\n        elif action == RecoveryAction.DOWNGRADE_ROLE:\n            current_role = self.role_map.get(model_id)\n            if current_role == Role.COL:\n                new_role = Role.PAR\n                self.role_map[model_id] = new_role\n\n        return action, new_role\n>>>>\nFOLLOW: This is an interface-breaking fix and must be propagated. (1) All callers of `get_recovery()` must unpack `(action, new_role)` and explicitly apply `new_role` to the authoritative `RoleAssignment` / load-balancing state; otherwise the downgrade remains partial. (2) Validator methods `validate_k1()` and any other direct callers must be updated to compare only the first tuple element. (3) If the codebase intentionally relied on shared-dict aliasing, this fix makes the dependency explicit and testable rather than implicit.",
